{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"b86ad11940be0c7575ee4ff2b621786c3f440086967c3fa53401e79158a61bcc","slug":"setting-up-full-multigrid","location":"/articles/setting-up-full-multigrid/setting-up-full-multigrid.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Setting Up Full Multigrid","description":"Underworld can precondition a Stokes solve with geometric multigrid built from a real hierarchy of meshes, rather than with the algebraic multigrid it falls back to. What the difference is, how to build a mesh that has a hierarchy, and when the choice is worth making.","keywords":["Underworld Code","Tricks of the Trade","development"],"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Louis Moresi","given":"Louis","family":"Moresi"},"name":"Louis Moresi","orcid":"0000-0003-3685-174X","affiliations":["Australian National University"],"id":"contributors-setting-up-full-multigrid-generated-uid-0"}],"affiliations":[{"id":"Australian National University","name":"Australian National University"}],"date":"2026-08-14","license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","free":true,"CC":true}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"setting-up-full-multigrid.md","url":"/underworldcode.org-preview/0c9cad6b66/build/setting-up-full-mult-11619621f668cfee6db0754f49087f8d.md"},{"format":"typst","filename":"setting-up-full-multigrid.pdf","url":"/underworldcode.org-preview/0c9cad6b66/build/setting-up-full-mult-9fef71c6f6bbd16810ecbc1f77fc0362.pdf"}],"open_access":true,"github":"https://github.com/underworld-technical-notes/underworldcode.org","source_url":"https://github.com/underworld-technical-notes/underworldcode.org/blob/main/articles/setting-up-full-multigrid/setting-up-full-multigrid.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/underworld-technical-notes/underworldcode.org/edit/main/articles/setting-up-full-multigrid/setting-up-full-multigrid.md"},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most of the time in a geodynamics model goes into solving the Stokes equations,\nand most of that goes into the velocity block. How that block is preconditioned\ndecides how long a model takes to run, and it is one of the few settings where\nthe right choice is worth an order of magnitude.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"UBAM5R93Pr"}],"key":"GWFBPVszIm"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What multigrid does","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"JDFojNaICc"}],"identifier":"what-multigrid-does","label":"What multigrid does","html_id":"what-multigrid-does","implicit":true,"key":"RtO4QFeQSD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"An iterative solver reduces error unevenly. Simple relaxation methods — the\nsmoothers — are good at removing error that varies rapidly from cell to cell,\nand poor at removing error that varies smoothly across the whole domain. The\nsmooth part is the expensive part: on a fine mesh it takes many sweeps to move\ninformation from one side of the model to the other.","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"MkHn1tu0hQ"}],"key":"euT1uX8Idj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multigrid removes that part somewhere else. Error that is smooth on a fine mesh\nis ","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"zRkT0Qr6zd"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"not","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"gi06cipA2g"}],"key":"KXqQRdlkb3"},{"type":"text","value":" smooth on a mesh twice as coarse, where it spans half as many cells, so\na few sweeps on the coarse mesh remove what the fine mesh could not. The method\nis a recursion: smooth on the fine mesh, transfer what is left to a coarser one,\nsolve there (by recursing again), transfer the correction back, smooth again.\nEach level handles the band of error it can see cheaply, and the work per\nunknown stops growing as the model gets bigger.","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"NCwziAww1Q"}],"key":"VeUlyvoEdo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":52,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two things have to be supplied: the coarse levels, and the operators that move\nbetween them.","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"CkoW4QmNRf"}],"key":"nOpaJSH9Rd"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two ways to build the coarse levels","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"LpOVxVfX1c"}],"identifier":"two-ways-to-build-the-coarse-levels","label":"Two ways to build the coarse levels","html_id":"two-ways-to-build-the-coarse-levels","implicit":true,"key":"eZWkqosyhn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Algebraic multigrid","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"lIIudjCML5"}],"key":"g2YosFPoN6"},{"type":"text","value":" builds them from the matrix. It inspects the operator’s\nconnectivity, groups unknowns that are strongly coupled, and constructs coarse\nlevels and transfer operators from that grouping alone. It needs nothing from\nthe mesh, which is why it works everywhere and is the sensible default. PETSc’s\nimplementation is GAMG, and it is what Underworld uses when it has nothing\nbetter.","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"JKPrjLOfvi"}],"key":"UTtCevMHCt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Geometric multigrid","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"YnXShVHEJJ"}],"key":"E4MolrrulY"},{"type":"text","value":" uses actual coarser meshes. If the fine mesh was built\nby refining a coarse one, those coarser meshes already exist, and the transfers\nfollow from the refinement relation: each fine node either coincides with a\ncoarse node or lies inside a known coarse cell. In PETSc this is ","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"XokDdz5Zb5"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pc_type=mg","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"QD2QvDizfT"},{"type":"text","value":",\nand run as a ","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"gCkjHbZC2m"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"full","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"CRQfrpedgw"}],"key":"E8u71l9JWC"},{"type":"text","value":" multigrid cycle — starting on the coarsest level and working\nup, rather than starting fine — it is what we call FMG.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"QCyVwVxf15"}],"key":"uljsfis24y"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":70,"column":1},"end":{"line":74,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The difference matters when the operator’s connectivity is a poor guide to the\ngeometry, which is exactly what a strong viscosity contrast produces. The\nalgebraic method sees a matrix whose couplings are dominated by the stiff\nregion and groups unknowns accordingly; the geometric method does not care,\nbecause it was told the grids.","position":{"start":{"line":70,"column":1},"end":{"line":70,"column":1}},"key":"SL8uPvNY9i"}],"key":"voJsAtaXh9"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Making a mesh that has a hierarchy","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"F2fpDAiAgk"}],"identifier":"making-a-mesh-that-has-a-hierarchy","label":"Making a mesh that has a hierarchy","html_id":"making-a-mesh-that-has-a-hierarchy","implicit":true,"key":"Y7oewUC8Ty"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":79,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mesh only carries a hierarchy if it was built with one. That is the\n","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"ePBGmfvWIs"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"refinement","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"yIW3smJzGJ"},{"type":"text","value":" argument:","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"ebJicaqHW7"}],"key":"WAtOHAomZm"},{"type":"code","lang":"python","value":"mesh = uw.meshing.UnstructuredSimplexBox(cellSize=0.05, refinement=2)\nlen(mesh.dm_hierarchy)     # 3: the base and two refinements","position":{"start":{"line":81,"column":1},"end":{"line":84,"column":1}},"key":"ebkPqOREGc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":89,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mesh you get back is the finest level. The coarser ones are kept alongside\nit, and they are what the preconditioner uses. Build the same mesh at\n","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"key":"pcKclSCor7"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cellSize=0.0125","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"key":"CcPB9ZHy70"},{"type":"text","value":" with no refinement and you get a mesh of about the same\nresolution with no hierarchy at all — and no geometric multigrid.","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"key":"e72Gy3q49e"}],"key":"gLUeGvLt7m"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The solver picks it up on its own:","position":{"start":{"line":91,"column":1},"end":{"line":91,"column":1}},"key":"WQfFajhxhr"}],"key":"cotlNuGGGp"},{"type":"code","lang":"python","value":"stokes = uw.systems.Stokes(mesh)\nstokes.preconditioner            # \"auto\" by default\nstokes.solve()","position":{"start":{"line":93,"column":1},"end":{"line":97,"column":1}},"key":"HdVKg51r75"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":106,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"auto\"","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"Jf96gqqI9R"},{"type":"text","value":" uses geometric multigrid when ","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"xnW4xVOa5D"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"len(mesh.dm_hierarchy) > 1","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"QudNXjlY7H"},{"type":"text","value":" and GAMG\nwhen it does not. You can also ask directly, with ","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"Oemgd612Xl"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"fmg\"","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"tB6om9GVFA"},{"type":"text","value":" or ","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"ZWbUrIeVSv"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"gamg\"","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"htdAO5yKxr"},{"type":"text","value":". Two\nbehaviours are deliberate: ","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"N3Z7fLIJDI"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"auto\"","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"giy95a2uge"},{"type":"text","value":" only ever adds geometric multigrid to an\nuntouched configuration, so it will not overwrite preconditioner options you\nhave set yourself; and where a request cannot be honoured, the reason is\nrecorded in ","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"LVNvmKqYET"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stokes.pc_fallbacks","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"slFK93wBEb"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of the solve quietly running on\nsomething else. ","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"LxXtxUgPyC"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stokes.preconditioner_settings","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"j9XvpMxYf0"},{"type":"text","value":" reports what was actually\napplied.","position":{"start":{"line":99,"column":1},"end":{"line":99,"column":1}},"key":"NLTg8syBcz"}],"key":"f1O96ii3Xi"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":108,"column":1},"end":{"line":108,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Curved boundaries have to be told they are curved","position":{"start":{"line":108,"column":1},"end":{"line":108,"column":1}},"key":"uIcjfeZyhI"}],"identifier":"curved-boundaries-have-to-be-told-they-are-curved","label":"Curved boundaries have to be told they are curved","html_id":"curved-boundaries-have-to-be-told-they-are-curved","implicit":true,"key":"eHocB3N2OF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":110,"column":1},"end":{"line":114,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refining a mesh puts new nodes at the midpoints of existing edges. On a\nstraight boundary that is fine. On a curved one it is not: the coarse mesh\napproximates a circle by a polygon, and the midpoint of a chord lies inside the\ncircle, not on it. Refine naively and every level keeps the coarsest level’s\npolygon, so the geometry never improves no matter how fine the mesh becomes.","position":{"start":{"line":110,"column":1},"end":{"line":110,"column":1}},"key":"ArHsgpeBsE"}],"key":"iwYXD2Ybnb"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":119,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Underworld’s curved meshes carry a refinement callback that snaps\nboundary-labelled nodes back onto the true surface after each refinement. For\nthe annulus it is a few lines — take the nodes labelled ","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"key":"BHl0KPDtNa"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Upper","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"key":"oxesDDMePI"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"key":"gU4B1B1DD6"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Lower","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"key":"aQtGYVC6Z3"},{"type":"text","value":" and\nrescale each to the correct radius:","position":{"start":{"line":116,"column":1},"end":{"line":116,"column":1}},"key":"SJk5q5gyoH"}],"key":"yGjGh16p3O"},{"type":"code","lang":"python","value":"coords[upper] *= radiusOuter / R[upper]\ncoords[lower] *= radiusInner / R[lower]","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"key":"PRLM9qOldb"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":126,"column":1},"end":{"line":129,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This happens automatically for the built-in meshes, so in normal use there is\nnothing to do. It matters if you build a mesh of your own with curved\nboundaries: without a callback of this kind the hierarchy is geometrically\nwrong, and the error does not go away with refinement.","position":{"start":{"line":126,"column":1},"end":{"line":126,"column":1}},"key":"n2C1Z66LbW"}],"key":"dgow9WT6aR"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":131,"column":1},"end":{"line":131,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What the coarse mesh leaves behind","position":{"start":{"line":131,"column":1},"end":{"line":131,"column":1}},"key":"vqvGtKLJ5t"}],"identifier":"what-the-coarse-mesh-leaves-behind","label":"What the coarse mesh leaves behind","html_id":"what-the-coarse-mesh-leaves-behind","implicit":true,"key":"MZaXiAuLI9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":133,"column":1},"end":{"line":137,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because the fine mesh is made by subdividing the coarse one, the coarse mesh’s\nown triangulation is still visible in it. Its edges survive as continuous lines\nacross the fine mesh, and its vertices remain places where an unusual number of\nelements meet. The pattern is a record of how the mesh was made rather than of\nthe problem being solved.","position":{"start":{"line":133,"column":1},"end":{"line":133,"column":1}},"key":"h4tnV4PT9M"}],"key":"BlxvjOF5I2"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":139,"column":1},"end":{"line":144,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mesh.relax()","position":{"start":{"line":139,"column":1},"end":{"line":139,"column":1}},"key":"FBwdz5PgrE"},{"type":"text","value":" moves the nodes to improve element shapes while keeping the\nresolution and the topology, which loosens that imprint. On a twice-refined box\nit lifts median element quality from 0.976 to 0.994. The gain is small there\nbecause a gmsh base mesh is already good; it is larger the worse the base is.\nRelaxation moves coordinates only, so the hierarchy survives it — that is t